(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the principle of four quadrants in engineering orthographic projections, detailing the convention for plane rotation and the positions of views in all four quadrants.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Quadrant System Geometry:) Tj
T*
(   The space is partitioned into four dihedral quadrants by the intersection of two mutually) Tj
T*
(   perpendicular infinite reference planes:) Tj
T*
(   - Horizontal Plane \(HP\): The horizontal reference datum.) Tj
T*
(   - Vertical Plane \(VP\): The vertical frontal reference datum.) Tj
T*
(   - Reference Line \(XY\): The line of intersection between HP and VP \(Ground line\).) Tj
T*
(   - Quadrant 1: Above HP, In front of VP.) Tj
T*
(   - Quadrant 2: Above HP, Behind VP.) Tj
T*
(   - Quadrant 3: Below HP, Behind VP.) Tj
T*
(   - Quadrant 4: Below HP, In front of VP.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Standard Plane Rotation Convention:) Tj
T*
(   To represent 3D orthogonal projections on a single 2D drawing sheet:) Tj
T*
(   - The Vertical Plane \(VP\) is maintained fixed in its vertical orientation.) Tj
T*
(   - The Horizontal Plane \(HP\) is rotated 90 degrees CLOCKWISE about the XY axis until it becomes) Tj
T*
(   perfectly coplanar with the VP.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Projections in Each Quadrant:) Tj
T*
(   - First Quadrant: Front view \(elevation\) is ABOVE XY; Top view \(plan\) is BELOW XY.) Tj
T*
(   - Second Quadrant: Front view is ABOVE XY; Top view rotates 90? clockwise upwards, also landing) Tj
T*
(   ABOVE XY \(views overlap, causing visual ambiguity\).) Tj
T*
(   - Third Quadrant: Front view is BELOW XY; Top view is ABOVE XY.) Tj
T*
(   - Fourth Quadrant: Front view is BELOW XY; Top view rotates downwards, also landing BELOW XY) Tj
T*
(   \(views overlap, causing visual ambiguity\).) Tj
T*
(   Due to the overlapping of views in the 2nd and 4th quadrants, engineering practice) Tj
T*
(   internationally is restricted strictly to 1st and 3rd angle projections.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State the fundamental rules governing the orthographic projection of points, explaining why distance from HP appears in the front view and distance from VP appears in the top view.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Fundamental Rules of Point Projection:) Tj
T*
(   - Rule 1: The projector connecting the front view \(a'\) and the top view \(a\) of any point A is) Tj
T*
(   always a single straight line perpendicular to the reference line XY.) Tj
T*
(   - Rule 2: The perpendicular distance of a point from the Horizontal Plane \(HP\) is displayed as) Tj
T*
(   the perpendicular distance of its front view \(a'\) from the XY line.) Tj
T*
(   - Rule 3: The perpendicular distance of a point from the Vertical Plane \(VP\) is displayed as) Tj
T*
(   the perpendicular distance of its top view \(a\) from the XY line.) Tj
T*
(   - Rule 4: The distance of a point from the Profile Plane \(PP\) is displayed as the distance of) Tj
T*
(   its side view \(a"\) from the profile reference axis X1Y1.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Geometric Proof of Distance Correspondence:) Tj
T*
(   - Consider point A in 3D space: Let its coordinates be \(x, y, z\), where z is height above HP) Tj
T*
(   and y is distance in front of VP.) Tj
T*
(   - Elevation on VP: Rays perpendicular to VP project point A onto VP. The height above the) Tj
T*
(   horizontal ground plane remains z. Thus, the vertical distance from elevation a' to the XY) Tj
T*
(   ground line is identically z \(distance from HP\).) Tj
T*
ET

