/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: A straight line AB of 80 mm length has its end A 15 mm above HP and 20 mm in front of VP. The line is inclined at 30 degrees to HP and 45 degrees to VP. Draw its projections using the rotating line method and determine its apparent lengths and apparent angles.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Analytical Derivation and Step-by-Step Construction:) Tj
T*
(   1. Given Data:) Tj
T*
(   - True Length TL = 80 mm) Tj
T*
(   - End A: 15 mm above HP \(a' is 15 mm above XY\), 20 mm in front of VP \(a is 20 mm below XY\)) Tj
T*
(   - True inclination with HP: theta = 30 degrees) Tj
T*
(   - True inclination with VP: phi = 45 degrees) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Calculation of Apparent Lengths & Coordinate Loci:) Tj
T*
(   - Elevation Length \(EL = a'b'\): EL = TL * cos\(phi\) = 80 * cos\(45?\) = 80 * 0.7071 = 56.57 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Plan Length \(PL = ab\): PL = TL * cos\(theta\) = 80 * cos\(30?\) = 80 * 0.8660 = 69.28 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Height of locus of b' above HP: z_b = 15 + TL * sin\(theta\) = 15 + 80 * sin\(30?\) = 15 + 40 =) Tj
T*
(   55 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Distance of locus of b from VP: y_b = 20 + TL * sin\(phi\) = 20 + 80 * sin\(45?\) = 20 + 56.57 =) Tj
T*
(   76.57 mm.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Step-by-Step Rotating Line Construction:) Tj
T*
(   - Step 1: Draw reference line XY. Mark projector of A: a' at 15 mm above XY, a at 20 mm below) Tj
T*
(   XY.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 2: Draw horizontal locus line of b' at 55 mm above XY, and locus line of b at 76.57 mm) Tj
T*
(   below XY.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 3: With a' as center and radius TL = 80 mm, draw a line inclined at 30? to XY meeting) Tj
T*
(   locus of b' at b1'. Project b1' down to XY line and to the horizontal locus of a to obtain b1.) Tj
T*
(   Length a-b1 = Plan Length \(PL\) = 69.28 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 4: With a as center and radius TL = 80 mm, draw a line inclined at 45? to XY meeting) Tj
T*
(   locus of b at b2. Project b2 up to the horizontal locus of a' to obtain b2'. Length a'-b2' =) Tj
T*
(   Elevation Length \(EL\) = 56.57 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 5: With a as center and radius PL = 69.28 mm, swing an arc to intersect the locus of b) Tj
T*
(   at final top view b. Join a-b.) Tj
T*
(   - Step 6: With a' as center and radius EL = 56.57 mm, swing an arc to intersect the locus of b') Tj
T*
(   at final front view b'. Join a'-b'. Check that b' and b lie on the exact same vertical) Tj
T*
(   projector!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Results:) Tj
T*
(   - Apparent elevation angle alpha = asin\(40 / 56.57\) = 45.0 degrees.) Tj
T*
(   - Apparent plan angle beta = asin\(56.57 / 69.28\) = 54.74 degrees.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 7:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: A straight line PQ has end P 20 mm above HP and 25 mm in front of VP. End Q is 65 mm above HP and 70 mm in front of VP. The distance between the end projectors is 50 mm. Determine the true length of PQ, its true inclinations with HP and VP, and locate its traces.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Given Data:) Tj
T*
(   - Point P: z_p = 20 mm \(above HP\), y_p = 25 mm \(in front of VP\)) Tj
T*
(   - Point Q: z_q = 65 mm \(above HP\), y_q = 70 mm \(in front of VP\)) Tj
T*
(   - Distance between end projectors \(D\) = 50 mm.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Coordinate-Based Mathematical Determination:) Tj
T*
(   - Delta Z \(height difference\) = 65 - 20 = 45 mm.) Tj
T*
(   - Delta Y \(distance difference\) = 70 - 25 = 45 mm.) Tj
T*
ET

